## The Experience

Picture two people actually inventing something together inside a shared space, experimenting, reading each other's intent, inferring meaning before the other finishes speaking, carrying full sensory bandwidth: touch, sight, smell, hearing. Most of what a conversation communicates isn't the words, which is why people read a room better in person than on a video call. Full Dive Virtual Reality (FDVR), done right, carries that channel instead of flattening it.

FDVR renders the environment directly to the nervous system through a non-invasive brain-computer interface instead of a screen. Vision, sound, and other sensory input are generated and delivered straight to the brain, while the same interface intercepts and suppresses real-world motor output. The body stays still and safe while the mind is somewhere else entirely.

The target specification: a 40W local node streaming a 16K-equivalent sensory field at 120 fps.

Physics and thermodynamics either permit that specification or they don't. Where they don't, the gap defines what has to be invented.

## Working Backwards

Start from the end state, not from the hardware that's easiest to build, and follow every requirement it implies until you hit a wall.

State the experience, determine what it physically requires, and follow each requirement until it resolves against known physics or reaches something that doesn't exist yet. That boundary is the research agenda.

This architecture targets the uncompromising version of the experience: full sensory bandwidth, not a scaled-down spec that's easier to ship. The physics demands components and architectures that don't yet exist at commercial scale.

Each part of the design below sits where it does because a physical limit left it nowhere else.

## The Architecture in Outline

The architecture turns on three power scales: roughly 190mW the head can dissipate, roughly 20mW that can be delivered to it wirelessly, and roughly 40W available to a compute node in the room. Each part of the system lands at the scale it does because it's pinned there by one of these three ceilings.

Why ~20mW? Exposure limits cap the strength of the field in the room, and a headpiece-sized receiver intercepts that field over only tens of square centimetres. What it can harvest is therefore on the order of tens of milliwatts, depending on that capture area and on how efficiently its energy-harvesting antenna turns the field into usable current. ~20mW is the representative base case used here.

The derivation runs as a single chain. Each step below is forced by the one above it.

