## The Body Sets the Ceiling

Working backwards begins at the point of contact. The experience requires an interface that reads from and writes to the nervous system without surgery, which puts the headpiece directly against the skull, and puts it inside a real thermal limit, though the limit turns out to be wide, not narrow.

The head can be modeled as four layers (scalp, skull, the cerebrospinal fluid around the brain, and brain tissue), each conducting heat inward while blood flow carries heat away. This is the standard tissue heat-transfer calculation. Solved for the most power a device can dissipate on the scalp before any tissue warms by more than a degree, it puts the non-invasive thermal ceiling at roughly 85 to 570mW across the 10 to 60cm² of scalp contact a headpiece could realistically use. At the 20cm² a compact headpiece would occupy, the ceiling is 190mW. Beyond that, tissue warms by more than a degree, which is the point at which the design stops being non-invasive in any meaningful sense.

